      "message": "From the comment in our original `prj.conf`:\n\n```\nCONFIG_PTHREAD_KEY allocates memory from kernel memory pool to store thread\nlocal data. BoringSSL stores one pointer per thread, so 64 bytes seems to be\nenough\n```\n\n[The upstream CL](https://crrev.com/c/6472692) that added `HEAP_MEM_POOL_ADD_SIZE_POSIX_THREADS`:\n\n```\n`pthread_setspecific` requires a stack in order to allocate the\n`struct pthread_key_data` data structure. On 64 bit systems this data\nstructure is 32 bytes, resulting in 160 bytes usage for the default 5\nsupported threads.\n```\n\nAre these two referring to the same thing (thread-specific data)? If so, we may not need to add a separate config for BoringSSL, since it would already be counted for in `HEAP_MEM_POOL_ADD_SIZE_POSIX_THREADS`.",
